{{Short description|National flag of Sinchew}}

The '''flag of [[Sinchew]]''' is the national flag of the [[Sinchew|Republic of Sinchew]]. It consists of a white field with two black triangular sections in the upper-left and lower-right corners, charged with a [[Baiduism|Baiduist]] knot in the centre. The flag was adopted in 1733 upon Sinchew's independence from the [[Kingdom of Zong]], following the [[Sinchewese Revolutionary War]] (1726–1733).

== Design ==

The flag is a rectangular banner with a proportion of 2:3 (height to width). The field is white. Two black right triangles occupy the upper-left and lower-right corners. The upper-left triangle has its right angle at the top-left corner of the flag and extends approximately two-fifths of the flag's width along the top edge and the full height along the left edge. The lower-right triangle is similarly proportioned, with its right angle at the lower-right corner. The two black triangles are diagonally opposed, creating a broad white diagonal band running from the lower-left to the upper-right.

At the centre of the flag is a [[Baiduism|Baiduist]] knot, an interlaced geometric symbol rendered in white with a black outline. The knot is a traditional Baiduist religious emblem representing the interconnectedness of all phenomena and the endless cycle of the Ocean Crossing (海渡), the central Baiduist teaching that likens spiritual practice to a voyage across the sea.

== Symbolism ==

The flag's design reflects the founding principles of the Republic of Sinchew, which established a secular state with a Baiduist majority population. The black-and-white colour scheme carries multiple layers of meaning. The absence of colour — the use of only black and white — represents the secular character of the state, distinguishing the republic from the [[Tianism|Tianist]] theocratic order of the [[Kingdom of Zong]], from which Sinchew gained its independence.

The two black triangles represent the twin forces of oppression that the [[Sinchewese Revolutionary War|revolution]] overthrew: the Zong imperial authority, which had subordinated Sinchew as a penal colony and denied it representation in the [[General Assembly]], and the [[Tianism|Tianist]] religious establishment, which had orchestrated the [[Baiduist Expulsion from Zong|expulsion]] of the Baiduist population and enforced religious persecution for over a century. The diagonally opposed placement of the triangles — one in the upper-left, one in the lower-right — evokes the two pillars of the old order pressing in from opposite sides, converging on the space where the new nation was forged. The white diagonal band that runs between them represents the revolutionary space — the freedom carved out between empire and theocracy by those who fought for independence.

The [[Baiduism|Baiduist]] knot at the centre of the flag carries both religious and revolutionary significance. As a religious emblem, the knot represents the interconnectedness of all phenomena and the endless cycle of the Ocean Crossing (海渡), the central Baiduist teaching that likens spiritual practice to a voyage across the sea. As a revolutionary symbol, the knot had been used in the insignia of the Sinchewese revolutionary forces during the [[Sinchewese Revolutionary War]] (1726–1733), where its endless, interlaced form came to represent the endless struggle of those who had fought for the freedom to practise their religion — a struggle that had begun with the expulsion in 1606 and continued through the colonial hardships, the [[Sinchew War]], and the revolutionary war itself. The knot's unbroken pattern served as a reminder that the fight for religious freedom was never truly finished, and that the liberty secured by the revolution required constant vigilance to maintain.

The knot, however, is rendered in the same black-and-white palette as the rest of the flag, rather than in any liturgical colour, reinforcing the secular framework within which the Baiduist heritage is acknowledged. The placement of the religious symbol within a secular colour scheme reflects the constitutional settlement of the republic: the state recognises and honours its Baiduist cultural heritage while maintaining a neutral posture toward all faiths.

The diagonal arrangement of the black triangles has also been read as a reference to the trans-oceanic journey of the Baiduist settlers who were expelled from Zong and transported to Sinchew beginning in 1606. The diagonal axis, running from the lower-left to the upper-right, suggests the trajectory of a voyage — the crossing of the [[Shendan Ocean]] that transformed a penal colony into an independent nation.

== History ==

=== Adoption ===

The flag was adopted in 1733, upon the conclusion of the [[Sinchewese Revolutionary War]] (1726–1733), in which Sinchew achieved independence from the [[Kingdom of Zong]]. The revolutionary government that emerged from the war established a republic founded on secular principles, despite the Baiduist faith being shared by the majority of the population.

The adoption of the flag represented a deliberate departure from the symbols of the Zong colonial administration. During the colonial period, Sinchew had flown a colonial flag consisting of a jade green field — the imperial colour of the [[Kingdom of Zong]] — charged with the imperial symbol of Zong alongside the two mantas, the colonial emblem of Sinchew. The colonial flag, adopted upon the founding of the colony at [[Tamsui]] in 1538, signified Sinchew's subordinate status within the Zong imperial framework: the imperial symbol asserted the authority of the Zong crown, while the two mantas acknowledged the colony's distinct maritime identity. After the [[Merchants' Revolution]] of 1643, the colonial flag remained in use alongside the constitutional banner of the Zong [[General Assembly]]. The new flag's black-and-white palette and its central Baiduist knot were chosen to signal the republic's complete break with both the Zong imperial framework and the colonial symbols that had represented Sinchew's subordination. The jade green of the colonial flag — the colour of the Zong crown — was replaced by the neutral black-and-white scheme of the republic, and the two mantas were replaced by the Baiduist knot, shifting the flag's central symbol from a colonial emblem to a religious and cultural one.

The flag was designed by [[Lîm Huat-tiat]] (林法哲, c. 1690–1745), a Baiduist monk and revolutionary intellectual who had participated in the [[Sinchewese Revolutionary War]] as a member of the revolutionary council. Lîm, born in [[Tamsui]] to a family of Baiduist settlers descended from those expelled from Zong in 1606, drew upon the religious and revolutionary iconography of the Baiduist community in composing the design. The Baiduist knot, which he placed at the centre of the flag, had been used in the insignia of the revolutionary forces during the war, where its endless, interlaced form had come to represent the unending struggle for religious freedom. Lîm's design was selected by the revolutionary government from among several proposals and was formally adopted upon the declaration of independence in 1733.

The selection of the Baiduist knot as the flag's central charge honoured the religious heritage that had sustained the Baiduist community through the expulsion, the colonial hardships, and the revolutionary war. Its placement within a secular colour scheme, rather than a religious one, reflected the constitutional principle that the republic would not be a theocracy but a secular state that recognised its Baiduist cultural foundations.

=== Context ===

The flag's design was shaped by the historical experience of the Sinchewese population. The [[Baiduist Expulsion from Zong]] (1606–1650) had transported over one million Zong Baiduists to the Sinchew colony, transforming a penal plantation settlement into a major population centre with a distinct cultural and religious identity. The [[Sinchew War]] (1648–1656) had further strained the relationship between the colony and the mainland, as the Baiduist settlers resisted Zong's attempts to reassert control. The [[First War of the Isthmus]] (1711–1716) had devastated the colony, with approximately 5,000 Sinchewese killed, including 3,000 enslaved persons impressed into military service. The post-war period brought further hardship: the 1717 landowners' rebellion was suppressed, and the 1718 emancipation decree, while abolishing slavery, triggered an economic collapse that the Zong court refused to alleviate. The accumulated grievances — economic devastation, political repression, and the colonial government's marginalisation within the Zong commonwealth — fuelled the [[Sinchewese Revolutionary War]] and the eventual adoption of a flag that symbolised the complete break with Zong.

== Colonial flag ==

The colonial flag of Sinchew, used from the founding of the colony at [[Tamsui]] in 1538 until independence in 1733, consisted of a jade green field charged with the imperial symbol of [[Kingdom of Zong|Zong]] and the two mantas of Sinchew, both rendered in white. The jade green background was the imperial colour of the Zong crown, used on the royal banner and associated with the [[House of Yuanlong]]. The imperial symbol, placed in the upper-left portion of the flag, asserted the sovereignty of the Zong crown over the colony. The two mantas, placed on the right portion of the flag, were the colonial emblem of Sinchew — a symbol derived from the marine life of the Sinchewese coast and adopted as the colony's identifying mark within the Zong colonial system.

The colonial flag was replaced by the current national flag upon independence in 1733. The two mantas, though no longer used on the national flag, remain a cultural symbol of Sinchew and appear in various civic and maritime contexts.

